where $x$ and $y$ are functions of $\\theta .$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$\\frac{1}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]^{6}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{6}} & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-6$ and $\\phi =\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-6}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-6\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\theta )\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\phi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\phi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )$ for any input $\\phi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-6\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )-[-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-325344/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc91582-8d44-5de1-9c98-1c5de173eefb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325344"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5271"] :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\left[ \\cos \\left( \\dfrac{\\theta}{10} \\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left( \\dfrac{\\theta}{10}\\right) \\right]^4.$" :question/worked-solution "De Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\left[ \\cos(\\varphi) + \\textrm{i}\\sin(\\varphi) \\right]^n = \\cos(n\\varphi) + \\textrm{i}\\sin{(n\\varphi)}.\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=4$ and $\\varphi=\\dfrac{\\theta}{10}.$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{10})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{10})]}^{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\theta }{10})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\theta }{10}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{5})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5270"] :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{1}{\\left[ \\cos (\\theta) +\\textrm{i}\\sin(\\theta) \\right]^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\left[ \\cos(\\theta) +\\textrm{i}\\sin(\\theta) \\right]^2} = \\left[ \\cos(\\theta) +\\textrm{i}\\sin(\\theta) \\right]^{-2} .\n$$\n\nDe Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\left[ \\cos(\\varphi) + \\textrm{i}\\sin(\\varphi) \\right]^n = \\cos(n\\varphi) + \\textrm{i}\\sin{(n\\varphi)}.\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-2$ and $\\varphi=\\theta.$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{2}} & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\cos(-\\varphi) = \\cos (\\varphi)$ and $\\sin(-\\varphi) = -\\sin(\\varphi)$ for any input $\\varphi,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos(-2\\theta) + \\textrm{i}\\sin(-2\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta) - \\textrm{i}\\sin{(2\\theta)}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5291"] :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{1}{\\left[ \\cos (2\\theta) -\\textrm{i}\\sin (2\\theta) \\right]^4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]}^{4}} & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\left[ \\cos (\\varphi) - \\textrm{i}\\sin(\\varphi) \\right]^n = \\cos(n\\varphi) - \\textrm{i}\\sin{(n\\varphi)}.\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-4$ and $\\varphi=2\\theta.$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]}^{-4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4\\cdot 2\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4\\cdot 2\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-8\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-8\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\cos(-\\varphi) = \\cos (\\varphi)$ and $\\sin(-\\varphi) = -\\sin(\\varphi)$ for any input $\\varphi,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-8\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-8\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )-[-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
